  "id": "Compound::DB00542 - Gene::6564",
  "verbalization": "Benazepril, a clinically used antihypertensive drug, is shown to bind to Slc15a2, a protein that participates in various transport activities, including proton-dependent peptide transport and proton/oligonucleotide cotransport. These activities are all mediated by Slc15a1, suggesting a functional link between Slc15a2 and Slc15a1 through shared transport mechanisms. The evidence indicates that Benazepril may influence Slc15a1 by modulating the activity of Slc15a2, which in turn affects the transport of peptides and nucleic acids across cellular membranes. The high scores in all three paths suggest a consistent and significant relationship, with the shared participation of Slc15a2 in transport processes that are ultimately dependent on Slc15a1. This integrated pathway highlights the role of Slc15a2 as a mediator in the drug's interaction with Slc15a1, potentially through a mechanism involving proton-dependent transport. The relationship is supported by the overlapping functional roles of Slc15a2 and Slc15a1, reinforcing the hypothesis that Benazepril may exert its effects via a transport-related mechanism involving Slc15a1.",
